Amazon EC2

### Q1. Your EC2 instance is running, but you cannot connect through SSH. How will you troubleshoot?

Spoken answer:

"First, I will check whether the EC2 instance is in the running state and whether its status checks are passed.

After that, I will verify the Security Group and check whether port 22 is allowed from my IP address.

Then I will check the Network ACL, route table, and Internet Gateway configuration.

I will also verify whether the instance has a public IP address and whether I am using the correct private key.

If everything looks correct, I will check the operating system firewall and SSH service.

Based on the issue, I will fix the configuration and try connecting again."

Important points:

- EC2 status checks.
- Security Group – port 22.
- NACL.
- Public IP.
- Route table.
- SSH key permissions.
- SSH service status.

### Q2. Your EC2 instance CPU utilization suddenly reaches 100%. What will you do?

Spoken answer:

"First, I will open CloudWatch and check the CPU utilization graph to understand when the issue started.

Then I will connect to the EC2 instance and check which process is consuming high CPU using commands like top or htop.

If it is caused by an application process, I will check the application logs and identify the root cause.

If traffic is suddenly increased, I can use Auto Scaling to launch additional instances and distribute traffic through a Load Balancer.

If the workload consistently requires more computing power, I can consider upgrading the EC2 instance type.

My main focus will be to identify the root cause instead of simply restarting the server."

### Q3. Your EC2 instance suddenly becomes unreachable. What will you do?

Spoken answer:

"First, I will check the EC2 instance state and system status checks from the AWS console.

If the instance is running but unreachable, I will investigate network connectivity, Security Groups, NACLs, and route tables.

I will also check whether the operating system is responding.

If SSH is not working, I can use EC2 Serial Console, if supported and enabled, or AWS Systems Manager Session Manager if the required configuration is available.

If it is an underlying infrastructure issue, I will follow the AWS recovery recommendations.

Before taking any destructive action, I will make sure that important data is protected."

### Q4. You have deployed an application on EC2, but users are getting a 502 Bad Gateway error. How will you troubleshoot?

Spoken answer:

"First, I will understand whether the issue is coming from the Load Balancer or the backend application.

I will check the Application Load Balancer target group and verify the health status of registered EC2 instances.

Then I will check the health check path, port, and protocol.

After that, I will connect to the backend server and verify whether the application service is running.

I will also check application logs, web server logs, and Security Group rules between the Load Balancer and EC2 instances.

Once I identify the issue, I will fix the backend application or network configuration and verify the response again."

### Q5. Your EC2 instance is running, but the website is not opening. What will you check?

Spoken answer:

"First, I will check whether the web server, such as Apache or Nginx, is running.

Then I will verify whether the application is listening on the correct port.

I will check the Security Group to make sure HTTP port 80 or HTTPS port 443 is allowed.

I will also verify the public IP, DNS configuration, and route table.

If the server is behind a Load Balancer, I will check target health and listener configuration.

Finally, I will check application logs to identify whether the issue is related to the application or infrastructure."

# 2. S3 – Real-Time Scenario Questions

