Amazon S3

### Q6. A user accidentally deletes an important file from an S3 bucket. How will you recover it?

Spoken answer:

"First, I will check whether S3 Versioning was enabled on that bucket.

If versioning is enabled, S3 maintains previous versions of objects.

I can identify the previous version of the deleted file and restore it.

If the object was deleted in a versioned bucket, it may have a delete marker, which can be removed to make the previous version visible again.

If versioning was not enabled, I will check whether a backup or replication copy is available.

That's why I recommend enabling versioning for important production buckets."

### Q7. Your application is unable to upload files to S3. How will you troubleshoot?

Spoken answer:

"First, I will check the application error message to understand whether it is an access issue or a connectivity issue.

Then I will verify the IAM Role or IAM Policy attached to the application.

I will make sure the required S3 permissions, such as PutObject, are available.

I will also check the bucket policy, encryption configuration, and whether the application is accessing the correct bucket.

If the bucket uses SSE-KMS encryption, I will verify the required KMS permissions as well.

After correcting the permissions or configuration, I will test the upload again."

### Q8. Your S3 storage cost is increasing every month. How will you reduce it?

Spoken answer:

"First, I will analyze the storage usage and identify which buckets and objects are consuming more space.

Then I will check whether old files, logs, and backups are still required.

I can configure S3 Lifecycle Policies to automatically move older objects to cheaper storage classes.

For example, frequently accessed files can remain in S3 Standard, while older files can move to Glacier storage classes.

I will also check incomplete multipart uploads and delete unnecessary data according to the retention policy.

This will help reduce storage costs without affecting business requirements."

### Q9. Your S3 bucket contains confidential company data. How will you secure it?

Spoken answer:

"First, I will keep S3 Block Public Access enabled unless there is a specific approved requirement for public access.

I will use IAM policies and bucket policies to provide access only to authorized users and applications.

I will enable encryption at rest using SSE-S3 or SSE-KMS depending on security requirements.

I can also enable versioning and access logging or CloudTrail data events for auditing.

For sensitive data, I will review cross-account access and enforce HTTPS using bucket policies.

My main objective is to prevent unauthorized access and protect company data."

# 3. VPC – Real-Time Scenario Questions

AWS VPC blue icon | Free SVG

Amazon VPC

### Q10. Your EC2 instance in a private subnet cannot access the internet. How will you troubleshoot?

Spoken answer:

"First, I will check whether a NAT Gateway is configured in the public subnet.

Then I will verify whether the NAT Gateway is available and has an Elastic IP address.

After that, I will check the private subnet route table and confirm that the default route points to the NAT Gateway.

I will also verify the public subnet route table to make sure it has a route to the Internet Gateway.

Finally, I will check the Network ACL and Security Group rules.

Once the routing configuration is correct, the private instance should be able to initiate outbound internet connections."

### Q11. An EC2 instance in a public subnet is not accessible from the internet. What could be the reason?

Spoken answer:

"There can be multiple reasons.

First, I will check whether the instance has a public IPv4 address or Elastic IP.

Then I will verify whether the subnet's route table has a default route pointing to the Internet Gateway.

I will check the Security Group to make sure the required port is allowed.

I will also verify the Network ACL and operating system firewall.

If all these configurations are correct, I will check whether the application or SSH service is actually running on the instance."

### Q12. Your application server is in a private subnet, but you need to connect to it for maintenance. How will you do that?

Spoken answer:

"I have multiple options depending on the security requirements.

My preferred approach is AWS Systems Manager Session Manager because it allows me to connect to the instance without opening inbound SSH access.

For this, the instance needs the appropriate IAM permissions, SSM Agent, and connectivity to Systems Manager endpoints.

Another option is to use a Bastion Host in a public subnet and connect to the private instance through it.

I would prefer Session Manager because it reduces the need for publicly exposed management ports."

### Q13. Two EC2 instances in the same VPC cannot communicate. What will you check?

Spoken answer:

"First, I will verify whether both instances are in the correct VPC and check their private IP addresses.

Then I will check their Security Groups to confirm that the required inbound traffic is allowed.

I will also verify the Network ACL rules because NACLs are stateless and need appropriate inbound and outbound rules.

After that, I will check route tables, subnet configurations, and operating system firewalls.

Finally, I will test connectivity using tools like ping, telnet, or nc depending on the protocol and network rules."

