### Q14. An EC2 instance needs access to an S3 bucket. How will you provide access securely?

Spoken answer:

"I will create an IAM Role with the required S3 permissions.

For example, if the EC2 instance only needs to read files from a particular bucket, I will provide the required read-only permissions for that bucket.

Then I will attach that IAM Role to the EC2 instance through an instance profile.

After that, the application running on EC2 can automatically obtain temporary credentials.

I will avoid storing AWS access keys directly inside the application or EC2 instance because it is not a secure approach."

### Q15. A developer says they are getting an Access Denied error while accessing an S3 bucket. How will you troubleshoot?

Spoken answer:

"First, I will check which IAM User or Role the developer is using.

Then I will verify the IAM permissions and confirm whether the required S3 actions are allowed.

After that, I will check the bucket policy and any explicit Deny statements.

I will also check whether permissions are restricted by an SCP, permissions boundary, or VPC endpoint policy.

If the bucket uses KMS encryption, I will verify the required KMS permissions.

Once I identify the permission issue, I will grant only the required access."

