### Q21. One EC2 instance behind the Load Balancer is unhealthy. What will you do?

Spoken answer:

"First, I will open the Target Group and check the health status of the unhealthy instance.

I will review the health check path, port, and protocol.

Then I will connect to the EC2 instance and verify whether the application is running and responding correctly.

I will also check Security Group rules between the Load Balancer and the EC2 instance.

If the instance has an application issue, I will fix it. If the instance is damaged or cannot be recovered, I will replace it through the Auto Scaling Group if configured."

### Q22. Your website traffic suddenly increases during a sale. How will you handle it?

Spoken answer:

"I will use an Application Load Balancer with an EC2 Auto Scaling Group.

The Load Balancer distributes incoming traffic across multiple healthy EC2 instances.

I will configure scaling policies based on metrics such as average CPU utilization or request count per target.

When traffic increases, Auto Scaling launches additional instances.

When traffic decreases, it terminates unnecessary instances according to the scaling configuration.

This helps maintain application performance and optimize infrastructure costs."

### Q23. Auto Scaling is launching new EC2 instances, but they are immediately terminating. What will you check?

Spoken answer:

"First, I will check the Auto Scaling Group Activity History to understand why the instances are terminating.

Then I will check EC2 system status checks and instance status checks.

I will also verify the Launch Template, AMI, instance type, subnet availability, and IAM permissions.

If the instances are failing Load Balancer health checks, I will check the health check grace period, application startup time, and health check configuration.

Based on the termination reason, I will fix the configuration and test the Auto Scaling Group again."

# 8. Route 53 – Real-Time Scenario Questions

Cloud Icons | Amazon Route 53 64@5x

Amazon Route 53

### Q24. Your website is working through its IP address, but the domain name is not opening. What will you check?

Spoken answer:

"First, I will check the DNS records in Route 53.

I will verify whether the A record or Alias record is pointing to the correct application endpoint.

Then I will check the Hosted Zone and confirm whether the domain registrar is using the correct Route 53 name servers.

I will also check DNS propagation and TTL settings.

If everything looks correct, I will use DNS troubleshooting commands like nslookup or dig to verify the DNS resolution."

### Q25. Your application is running in two AWS regions. How will you route users to a healthy region?

Spoken answer:

"I can use Route 53 Failover Routing or Latency-Based Routing depending on the business requirement.

For disaster recovery, I can configure Failover Routing with a primary and secondary application endpoint.

I will configure health checks to monitor the primary endpoint.

If the primary endpoint becomes unhealthy, Route 53 can return the secondary endpoint for DNS queries according to the failover configuration.

This helps improve application availability across regions."

