Spoken answer:

"Suppose I need to host a highly available web application in AWS.

First, I will create a VPC with public and private subnets across at least two Availability Zones.

In the public subnets, I will deploy an Application Load Balancer to receive incoming user traffic.

In the private application subnets, I will deploy EC2 instances using an Auto Scaling Group.

The Load Balancer will distribute traffic across healthy application instances.

For the database layer, I will use Amazon RDS with Multi-AZ deployment to provide high availability.

I will use Security Groups to control communication between the Load Balancer, application servers, and database.

For monitoring, I will configure CloudWatch metrics and alarms.

For DNS management, I will use Route 53.

For application backups and static files, I can use Amazon S3.

With this architecture, I can achieve better availability, scalability, security, and reliability."

